389:
(a) It shall be unlawful for any person to erect or cause to be erected any temporary structure or tent to be used for public assemblages or for public religious, educational or entertainment purposes without first obtaining a permit from the mayor and council. Any person desiring to erect such a tent or structure shall apply in writing for a permit. The application shall be delivered to the city clerk and shall reveal the following:
(1) The type of structure or tent desired;
(2) The material from which the tent or structure is made;
(3) The equipment expected to be used inside and outside the structure when erected;
(4) The exact use to be made of the structure;
(5) The number of people expected to use the facility;
(6) The arrangements for traffic control;
(7) Parking;
(8) The desired location of the structure;
(9) The precautions to be taken against fire; and
(10) The sanitary facilities available.
(b) The mayor and council shall consider the application within ten days of its submission and shall render a decision within 15 days. The mayor and council shall consider all pertinent factors, including but not limited to noise, dust, safety, traffic and nuisance, if any, to other citizens and property owners.
(c) A permit shall not be issued unless the proposed structure or tent conforms to the applicable sections of this chapter, the zoning regulations in chapter 42, all sanitary and health regulations and all other sections of this Code and other ordinances or unless the structure or tent, as finally constructed, complies with plans upon which the permit was requested.
(d) No temporary structure or tent shall ever be used as a residence.
(e) Permits for temporary structures shall expire 90 days after issuance, unless issued for a shorter period of time by vote of the mayor and council. One renewal may be granted by the mayor and council following expiration of the original permit term.
(f) Nothing in this section shall be construed as interfering with the use of tents for funeral purposes, Boy Scout activities and children's at-home recreation.
(Code 1972, § 4-8; Code 1994, § 18-8)

414:
(a) No plumbing fixture shall be installed which does not meet the standards listed in this section or the state minimum plumbing code, whichever is stricter. This includes all plumbing fixtures installed in newly constructed buildings or when replacing plumbing fixtures during remodeling or renovation of existing buildings, except as noted in section 10-126.
(b) The standards are as follows:
(1) A water closet or toilet that:
a. Is a dual-flush water closet that meets the following standards:
1. The average flush volume of two reduced flushes and one full flush may not exceed 1.28 gallons;
2. The toilet meets the performance, testing, and labeling requirements prescribed by the following standards, as applicable:
(i) American Society of Mechanical Engineers Standard A112.19.2-2008; and
(ii) American Society of Mechanical Engineers Standard A112.19.14-2006 Six-Liter Water Closets Equipped with a Dual Flushing Device"; and
3. Is listed to the WaterSense TM Tank-Type High Efficiency Toilet Specification; or
b. Is a single-flush water closet, including gravity, pressure-assisted, and electro-hydraulic tank types, that meets the following standards:
1. The average flush volume may not exceed 1.28 gallons;
2. The toilet must meet the performance, testing, and labeling requirements prescribed by the American Society of Mechanical Engineers Standard A112.192/CSA B45.1 or A112.19.14; and
3. The toilet must be listed to the WaterSense TM Tank-Type High Efficiency Toilet Specification;
(2) A showerhead that allows a flow of no more than an average of 2.5 gallons of water per minute at 60 pounds per square inch of pressure;
(3) A urinal and associated flush valve that:
a. Uses no more than 0.5 gallons of water per flush;
b. Meets the performance, testing, and labeling requirements prescribed by the American Society of Mechanical Engineers Standard A112.19.2/CSA B45.1;
c. For flushing urinals, meets all WaterSense TM specifications for flushing urinals; and
d. Where non-water urinals are employed, complies with American Society of Mechanical Engineers Standard A112.19.3/CSA B45.4 or American Society of Mechanical Engineers Standard A112.19.19/CSA B45.4. Non-water urinals shall be cleaned and maintained in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions after installation. Where non-water urinals are installed they shall have a water distribution line roughed-in to the urinal location at a minimum height of 56 inches (1,422 mm) to allow for the installation of an approved backflow prevention device in the event of a retrofit. Such water distribution lines shall be installed with shut-off valves located as close as possible to the distributing main to prevent the creation of dead ends. Where non-water urinals are installed, a minimum of one water-supplied fixture rated at a minimum of one water-supplied fixture unit shall be installed upstream on the same drain line to facilitate drain line flow and rinsing;
(4) A lavatory faucet or lavatory replacement aerator that allows a flow of no more than 1.5 gallons of water per minute at a pressure of 60 pounds per square inch in accordance with American Society of Mechanical Engineers Standard A112.18.1/CSA B.125.1 and listed to the WaterSense TM High-Efficiency Lavatory Faucet Specification; and
(5) A kitchen faucet or kitchen replacement aerator that allows a flow of no more than 2.0 gallons of water per minute.
(Code 1994, § 18-85; Ord. No. 91-1, § 3, 3-11-1991)

596:
If a manmade or natural disaster, actual enemy attack upon the United States or any other emergency occurs which may affect the lives and property of the citizens of the county and participating cities, the chairperson of the county board of commissioners, jointly with the mayors of the affected cities or, in their absences, their legally appointed designees, may determine that an emergency or disaster exists and thereafter shall have and may exercise for such period as such emergency or disaster exists or continues the power to:
(1) Enforce all rules, laws and regulations relating to emergency management and to assume direct operational control over all emergency management resources;
(2) Seize or take for temporary use any private property for the protection of the public;
(3) Sell, lend, give or distribute all or any such property or supplies among the inhabitants of the county and maintain a strict accounting of property or supplies distributed and for funds received for such property or supplies; and
(4) Perform and exercise such other functions and duties and take such emergency actions as may be necessary to promote and secure the safety, protection and well-being of the inhabitants of the county.
(Code 1994, § 26-31; Res. No. 91-4, § IV, 10-14-1991)

773:
(a) A nuisance is anything that works hurt, inconvenience or damage to another, and the fact that the act done may otherwise be lawful shall not keep it from being a nuisance. The inconvenience complained of shall not be fanciful, or such as would affect only one of fastidious taste, but it shall be such as would affect an ordinarily reasonable person.
(b) The following conditions, among others, may be declared to be nuisances:
(1) Stagnant water on premises;
(2) Any dead or decaying matter, weeds, brush, vegetation, or any fruit, vegetable, animal or rodent, or any other objectionable, unsightly, or unsanitary matter upon a premises which is odorous or capable of causing disease to the inhabitants of the city. For the purpose of this article, the term "weeds" means any brush, grass, vegetation, or any plant that is not regularly cultivated, that exceeds a height of ten inches; provided, however, weeds shall not include plants in a maintained wildflower garden. Any plant that is not regularly cultivated exceeding ten inches in height shall be presumed to be a weed. The term "regularly cultivated" means any plant which is routinely cared for by any person to foster its growth, including ornamental grasses, bushes, and trees;
(3) The pollution of public water or the injection of matter into the sewage system which would be damaging thereto;
(4) Maintaining a dangerous or diseased animal or fowl;
(5) Obstruction of a public street, highway or sidewalk without a permit;
(6) Unreasonably loud, disturbing and unnecessary noise;
(7) All walls, trees and buildings that may endanger persons or property;
(8) Uncleanliness;
(9) Unused or inoperable appliances of any size or type, including, but not limited to, iceboxes, refrigerators, washers, dryers, water heaters, televisions, stereos, or any parts thereof;
(10) Any trees, shrubbery or other plants, or parts thereof, which obstruct clear, safe vision on roadways and intersections of the city;
(11) Junked or abandoned motor vehicles;
(12) The generation of smoke or fumes in sufficient amount to cause odor or annoyance to the inhabitants of the city;
(13) Toilets or sanitary facilities, plumbing, or sewers in bad repair;
(14) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents, or other calamities;
(15) Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities;
(16) Dilapidation;
(17) Disrepair;
(18) Structural defects;
(19) Vacant and dilapidated dwellings, buildings, or structures being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes and/or other illegal activity; and
(20) Any other condition constituting a nuisance under state, local or federal law.
(Ord. No. 2013-01, § I(art. II, § 50-26), 4-8-2013; Ord. No. 2018-04, § 1, 6-11-2018)
State Law reference— Power of municipality to define and declare nuisances and to cause their removal or abatement by summary proceedings or otherwise without limitation or impairment by provisions of state law, O.C.G.A. § 41-2-9.

802:
(a) Except as provided in subsection (b) of this section, whenever any building, dwelling or structure is vacant for more than 60 days and is in violation of this article, the owner, shall within ten days of written notice from the city of the existence of a vacant building, dwelling or structure, register, or caused to be registered, such building, dwelling or structure as a vacant building, dwelling or structure and submit a vacant building plan.
(b) Any building, dwelling or structure designed as a home dwelling that is vacant for a period less than 12 months shall be exempt from registration under this section, provided that the building, dwelling or structure is currently and continuously being marketed for sale and is not otherwise in violation of this article. At any time such building, dwelling or structure ceases to be marketed for sale or at such time as the building, dwelling or structure becomes vacant for a period greater than 12 months, then the owner of such building, dwelling or structure, shall within ten days of such event, register, or caused to be registered such building, dwelling or structure as a vacant building, dwelling or structure and submit a vacant building plan.
(c) The vacant building registration shall be completed through and approved by the code enforcement officer accompanied with a filing fee. Registration of a vacant building shall be valid for a period of six months. The filing fee shall be in an amount as established by the mayor and council from time to time.
(d) If the building is vacant at the expiration of any registration period and the requirements of the vacant building plan have not been completed, the owner shall re-register such building and pay another filing fee. If the building is vacant at the expiration of any registration period and the requirements of the vacant building have been completed, the owner shall re-register such building, but shall not file a vacant building plan and shall not pay the filing fee.
(e) The owner registering a vacant building shall supply the following information on an authorized from provided by the city:
(1) Name, address, and telephone number of the owner;
(2) Name, address and telephone number of any responsible party, if applicable;
(3) Name, address, and telephone number of any local agent or representative of the owner;
(4) Name, address, and telephone number of all persons with any legal interest in the property, building or premises, including mortgagees and successors in interest;
(5) Legal description and tax parcel identification number of the premises on which the building is situated;
(6) The common address of the building;
(7) Date on which the building became vacant; and
(8) Vacant building plan.
(Ord. No. 12-03, § 1(19-1), 3-12-2012)

803:
(a) When a building is registered as required by this article, the owner shall submit, or caused to be submitted, for approval a vacant building plan. The plan shall contain the following:
(1) The reasonably expected period of vacancy;
(2) A plan to make the building ready for occupancy with sufficient detail regarding the proposed repair and/or rehabilitation of the building to enable the code enforcement officer to determine the adequacy of such plan;
(3) A letter of written consent by the owner allowing city officials to enter and inspect the property for the period in which the vacant building plan is in effect;
(4) For buildings, dwellings or structures which are identified as being or containing public nuisances, then the vacant building plan shall contain a plan of action to remedy such public nuisances;
(5) For each required plan, a time schedule shall be submitted identifying a date of commencement of repair and/or rehabilitation and schedule for completion of repair and/or rehabilitation for each building, dwelling or structure and identified nuisance;
(6) When the owner proposes to occupy, sell, lease or demolish the vacant building, then the owner shall submit a plan and time schedule for such action; and
(7) A plan of action to secure, monitor and maintain the building and premises thereof in conformance with this article.
(b) The code enforcement officer is hereby authorized to collect a fee to the enable city to monitor or inspect the building and to review and approve the vacant building plan. The code enforcement officer shall have discretion to determine the number of inspections required to monitor the subject building upon submission of the plan based upon the type and condition of the property and the history of violations at the subject property. A standard schedule of fees shall be published by the code enforcement officer.
(c) The vacant building plan shall be recorded and indexed in the property records of the county and shall be released by the city when it has been determined that the property is no longer vacant.
(d) Failure by the owner to submit a vacant building plan or comply with a vacant building plan that has been approved by the city under this section or pay a fee as required under this section is a violation of this article and may be remedied by any of the following:
(1) An administrative penalty of up to $500.00 for the first citation; up to $750.00 for the second citation; and up to $1,000.00 for the third citation and any others after that; or
(2) Acquisition of the property by eminent domain in a manner consistent with section 20-138.
(e) If the property is subject to a vacant building plan and the plan has been properly recorded in the property records of the county, the transferee is subject to the terms of the vacant building plan. The transferee may apply to the city to be released from the requirements of the vacant building plan.
(Ord. No. 12-03, § 1(19-2), 3-12-2012)

856:
It shall be unlawful for any person to engage in disorderly conduct within the corporate limits of the city. A person commits the offense of disorderly conduct when such person does any of the following:
(1) Acts in a violent or tumultuous manner toward another person whereby such person is placed in reasonable fear of the safety of such person's life, limb, or health;
(2) Acts in a violent or tumultuous manner toward another person whereby the property of such person is placed in danger of being damaged or destroyed;
(3) Without provocation, uses to or of another person in such other person's presence, opprobrious or abusive words which by their very utterance tend to incite to an immediate breach of the peace (that is to say, words which as a matter of common knowledge and under ordinary circumstances will, when used to or of another person in such other person's presence, naturally tend to provoke violent resentment, that is, words commonly called "fighting words");
(4) Without provocation, uses obscene and vulgar or profane language in the presence of or by telephone to a person under the age of 14 years which threatens an immediate breach of the peace; or
(5) Recklessly or knowingly commits any act which may reasonably be expected to prevent or disrupt a lawful meeting, gathering or procession.
(Code 1994, § 66-1; Ord. No. 91-02, §§ 1, 2, 6-10-1990; Ord. No. 98-04, 10-12-1998)

879:
(a) Use of grounds and facilities in general. It shall be unlawful for any person, firm, association, or corporation using the city parks, ball fields, facilities and grounds to fail to clean up debris, extinguish all fires when fires are permitted and leave the premises in as good an order and the facilities in as neat and sanitary condition as when they began use of facilities.
(b) Prohibited acts. It shall be unlawful for any person, firm, association, or corporation using the city parks, ball fields, facilities or grounds to either perform or permit to be performed any of the following acts:
(1) Willfully mar, deface, disfigure, injure, tamper with, displace or remove any buildings, tables, benches, signs, notices, placards, permanent monuments, statues, boundary markers, structures, fixtures, equipment, facilities, park property or appurtenances thereof whatsoever, either real or personal;
(2) Damage, cut, carve, transplant, or remove any tree or plant or any part thereof;
(3) Bring in, dump, deposit or leave any bottles, broken glass, ashes, paper boxes, cans, dirt, rubbish, waste, garbage, refuse, or other trash. No such refuse or trash shall be placed in storm sewers, drains, waters or contiguous to any park, or left anywhere on the grounds thereof, but shall be placed in the proper receptacles where these are provided, and where not provided all rubbish or waste shall be carried away from the park by the person responsible for the presence of such rubbish or waste, and properly disposed of elsewhere;
(4) Disturb the peace, or use any profane or obscene language;
(5) Endanger the safety of any person by any conduct or act;
(6) Take part in or abet the playing of any game involving thrown or otherwise propelled objects, such as balls, stones, arrows, javelins, or model airplanes, except in areas set apart for such forms of recreation. The playing of rough or potentially dangerous games such as football, basketball, handball, and soccer is prohibited, except on fields, courts, or areas provided therefor;
(7) Vend, sell, peddle, or offer for sale any commodity or article within any park area or facility, unless authorized in writing by the city;
(8) Bring any dangerous animal or any dog into any park area except as provided by special permit when such activity is part of an approved special event;
(9) Ride or cause to be ridden any horse, mule, or pony on any property in the park areas except as provided by special permit when such activity is part of an approved special event;
(10) Drive or park any automobile or off-road vehicle, except on a street, driveway, or parking lot in any park; or to park or leave any such vehicle on any place other than one established for public parking;
(11) Set up tents, shacks, or other temporary shelter for the purpose of overnight camping, nor shall any person leave any movable structure or special vehicle to be used, or that could be used for such purposes, such as a house trailer, camp trailer, wagon, or the like in the park area after closing hours, unless authorized by the city;
(12) To tamper with, break into or enter any facility or park area that is secured by locks or padlocks;
(13) To have any motorized vehicle or bicycle on any jogging track or ball field;
(14) To carry or use firearms in any park;
(15) Throw, discharge or otherwise place or caused to be placed in the waters of any fountain, pond, lake, stream, or other body of water in or adjacent to any park or any tributary, stream, storm sewer or drain flowing into such waters, any substance, matter, or thing, liquid or solid, which will or may result in the pollution of such waters;
(16) Build or attempt to build a fire, except in such areas and under such regulations as may be designated by the city. No person shall drop, throw, or otherwise scatter lighted matches, burning cigarettes or cigars, tobacco paper or other inflammable material within any park area or on any highway, road, or street abutting or contiguous thereto;
(17) Have in his possession, custody or control, or consume any alcoholic beverage, drug or any illegal substance, unless as otherwise allowed by resolution of the mayor and council;
(18) Barbecue anywhere other than in barbecue pits provided by the city or in areas designated for the use of portable above-ground grills;
(19) Paste, glue, tack or otherwise post any sign, placard, advertisement or inscription whatsoever, erect or cause to be erected any sign whatsoever on any public lands, highways, or roads adjacent to a park; and
(20) Perform any act which is prohibited by this Code.
(Code 1994, § 70-2; Ord. No. 96-4, § 1, 8-22-1996; Ord. No. 96-05, § 1, 8-22-1996)

